  "nl_text": "The schemabench_0022 database is a comprehensive system for managing user authentication, permissions, and application content. At its core, it contains a table called auth_group, which stores information about different groups of users, with each group identified by a unique id and a descriptive name. The auth_group_permissions table links these groups to specific permissions, which are stored in the auth_permission table, where each permission is assigned a unique id, a name, and a codename. Permissions can be associated with different content types, which are stored in the django_content_type table, allowing the system to manage permissions for various types of content. Users are stored in the auth_user table, with each user having a unique id, a password, and a range of attributes such as their first and last name, email address, and whether they are staff or active. The auth_user_groups table links users to the groups they belong to, while the auth_user_user_permissions table links users to the permissions they have been granted. The django_admin_log table tracks changes made to the system, including the user who made the change, the type of change, and a description of the change. The django_migrations table keeps track of the different migrations that have been applied to the database, and the django_session table stores information about user sessions, including a unique session key, the data stored in the session, and the date when the session expires.",
  "gold_question": "1) Background / Context\n\nDjango is a high-level Python web framework designed to facilitate rapid development and clean, pragmatic design. It offers a comprehensive platform for developers to build web applications without the need to reinvent common components. Django streamlines web development, provides robust security features, and offers scalability for high-traffic applications.\n\n2) Objectives\n\nThe primary goal is to provide a framework that supports fast development of web applications, ensuring a secure and scalable environment. Django aims to handle common web development tasks efficiently, allowing developers to focus on creating the core functionality of their applications.\n\n3) Stakeholders & Users\n\n- Developers: Utilize Django to expedite the development process of web applications.\n- Administrators: Manage user accounts, permissions, and additional backend tasks.\n- End-users: Interact with web applications built on the Django framework.\n\n4) Functional Descriptions\n\n- Rapid Development: Facilitate quick project turnaround from concept to deployment.\n- Comprehensive Features: Include user authentication, content management, site maps, RSS feeds, and more.\n- Security: Protect against SQL injections, cross-site scripting, and other common vulnerabilities.\n- User Management: Support secure user authentication and permission management through a robust admin interface.\n- Data Logging: Track changes and activities within the system using the Django admin log.\n\n5) Non-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Scalability: Support scaling to meet high traffic demands efficiently.\n- Flexibility: Allow versatile application design, suitable for various types of projects from CMS to social networks.\n- Open Source: Provide a free and community-supported framework, encouraging contributions and enhancements.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_llama31/0022.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0022\nCREATE TABLE `auth_group` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `auth_permission` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `codename` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `django_content_type` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `app_label` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `model` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `auth_user` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `username` varchar(150) NOT NULL,\n  `password` varchar(128) NOT NULL,\n  `first_name` varchar(30) NOT NULL,\n  `last_name` varchar(30) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(254) NOT NULL,\n  `is_staff` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '0',\n  `is_active` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '1',\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `auth_group_permissions` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `group_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `permission_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `auth_group_permissions_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`group_id`) REFERENCES `auth_group` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `auth_group_permissions_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`permission_id`) REFERENCES `auth_permission` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `auth_user_groups` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `group_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `auth_user_groups_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `auth_user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `auth_user_groups_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`group_id`) REFERENCES `auth_group` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `auth_user_user_permissions` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `permission_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `auth_user_user_permissions_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `auth_user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `auth_user_user_permissions_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`permission_id`) REFERENCES `auth_permission` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `django_admin_log` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `action_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `content_type_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `object_id` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `object_repr` varchar(200) NOT NULL,\n  `action_flag` smallint(6) NOT NULL,\n  `change_message` text NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `django_admin_log_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `auth_user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `django_admin_log_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`content_type_id`) REFERENCES `django_content_type` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `django_migrations` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `app` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `applied` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `django_session` (\n  `session_key` varchar(40) NOT NULL,\n  `session_data` text NOT NULL,\n  `expire_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`session_key`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
